"arXiv:quant-ph/9508027v2  25 Jan 1996 (https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9508027)\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 28; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view2\",\"pattern\":\"power of a prime\"}); Total lines: 1862\nL75@P2: architecture with which a quantum computer is built. It is currently not clear which\nL76@P2: architectures, if any, will give high precision, and what this precision will be. If the pre\u0002cision of a quantum computer is large enough to make it more powerful than a classical\nL77@P2: computer, then in order to understand its potential it is important to think of precision\nL78@P2: as a resource that can vary. Treating the precision as a large constant (even though it is\nL94@P2: It thus seems plausible that the natural computing power of classical mechanics corre\u0002sponds to Turing machines,1 while the natural computing power of quantum mechanics\nL95@P2: might be greater.\nL96@P2: 1\nL97@P2: I believe that this question has not yet been settled and is worthy of further investigation. See\nL98@P2: Vergis et al. [1986], Steiglitz [1988], and Rubel [1989]. In particular, turbulence seems a good candidate\nL99@P2: for a counterexample to the quantitative Church’s thesis because the non-trivial dynamics on many\nL100@P2-3: length scales may make it difficult to simulate on a classical computer.4 P. W. SHOR\nL101@P3: The first person to look at the interaction between computation and quantum me\u0002chanics appears to have been Benioff [1980, 1982a, 1982b]. Although he did not ask\nL102@P3: whether quantum mechanics conferred extra power to computation, he showed that re\u0002versible unitary evolution was sufficient to realize the computational power of a Turing\nL103@P3: machine, thus showing that quantum mechanics is at least as powerful computationally\nL104@P3: as a classical computer. This work was fundamental in making later investigation of\nL105@P3: quantum computers possible.\nL106@P3: Feynman [1982,1986] seems to have been the first to suggest that quantum mechanics\nL107@P3: might be more powerful computationally than a Turing machine. He gave arguments as\nL108@P3: to why quantum mechanics might be intrinsically expensive computationally to simulate\nL109@P3: on a classical computer. He also raised the possibility of using a computer based on\nL110@P3: quantum mechanical principles to avoid this problem, thus implicitly asking the converse\nL111@P3: question: by using quantum mechanics in a computer can you compute more efficiently\nL112@P3: than on a classical computer? Deutsch [1985, 1989] was the first to ask this question\nL113@P3: explicitly. In order to study this question, he defined both quantum Turing machines\nL114@P3: and quantum circuits and investigated some of their properties.\nL115@P3: The question of whether using quantum mechanics in a computer allows one to\nL116@P3: obtain more computational power was more recently addressed by Deutsch and Jozsa\nL117@P3: [1992] and Berthiaume and Brassard [1992a, 1992b]. These papers showed that there\nL118@P3: are problems which quantum computers can quickly solve exactly, but that classical\nL119@P3: computers can only solve quickly with high probability and the aid of a random number\nL120@P3: generator. However, these papers did not show how to solve any problem in quantum\nL121@P3: polynomial time that was not already known to be solvable in polynomial time with\nL122@P3: the aid of a random number generator, allowing a small probability of error; this is\nL123@P3: the characterization of the complexity class BPP, which is widely viewed as the class of\nL124@P3: efficiently solvable problems.\nL125@P3: Further work on this problem was stimulated by Bernstein and Vazirani [1993]. One\nL126@P3: of the results contained in their paper was an oracle problem (that is, a problem involving\nL127@P3: a “black box” subroutine that the computer is allowed to perform, but for which no 