: cite21†Image†media.springernature.com L53: \nL54: ### cite22†Improving the Success Probability for Shor’s Factorization Algorithm L55: \nL56: Chapter © 2018\nL57: \nL58: cite23†Image†media.springernature.com L59: \nL60: ### cite24†Factoring semi-primes with (quantum) SAT-solvers L61: \nL62: Article Open access 14 May 2022\nL72: There exist efficient probabilistic primality tests, such as Miller–Rabin [cite26†15 , cite27†18 ], and efficient algorithms for reducing perfect powers \\(z = q^e\\) to q: A simple option is to test if \\(z^{1/d}\\) is an integer for some \\(d \\in [2, \\lfloor \\log z \\rfloor ]\\). For more advanced options, see e.g. Bernstein et al. [cite28†1 ].\nL114: This is closely related to the observations made in this paper. The original algorithm is from Miller [cite26†15 ].\nL119: ## 3 Our contribution\nL120: \nL121: We give an efficient classical probabilistic polynomial-time algorithm, that is essentially due to Miller [cite26†15 ], for completely factoring N given the order r of a single element g selected uniformly at random from \\({\\mathbb {Z}}_N^*\\). We furthermore analyze the runtime and success probability of the algorithm: In particular, we give a lower bound on its success probability.\nL127: ### 3.2 The algorithm\nL128: \nL186: Note that the order-finding call in step 1 may be performed for a multiple of N if desired. This can only cause r to grow by some multiple, which in turn can only serve to increase the success probability, in the same way that growing r to \\(r'\\) in step 2 serves to increase the success probability, see Sect. cite48†3.3 . In turn, this explains why we can replace N with \\(N'\\) as described in the previous section, and why a restriction to odd N does not imply a loss of generality.\nL187: #### 3.2.3 Notes on analogies with Miller’s, Rabin’s and Long’s works\nL188: \nL189: Miller’s original version of the algorithm in Sect. cite47†3.2 is deterministic, and proven to work only assuming the validity of the extended Riemann hypothesis (ERH), as is Miller’s primality test in the same thesis [cite26†15 ].\nL190: Rabin [cite27†18 ] later converted Miller’s primality test into a probabilistic polynomial-time algorithm that is highly efficient in practice. At about the same time, Long [cite49†13 ], acknowledging ideas from Flajolet, converted Miller’s factoring algorithm into a probabilistic polynomial-time algorithm.\nL191: This in a technical report that seemingly went unpublished.^{cite50†Footnote 1 } More specifically, Long lower-bounds the probability of randomly selecting an element \\(g \\in \\mathbb Z_N^*\\) of order r a multiple of \\(\\lambda '(N)\\). He then gives a randomized version of Miller’s algorithm for splitting N into two non-trivial factors given this multiple of \\(\\lambda '(N)\\).\nL192: The above is closely related to our work. We take matters a step further, however, by converting Miller’s factoring algorithm into an efficient probabilistic polynomial-time algorithm for recovering the complete factorization of N given the order r of a single element g selected uniformly at random from \\(\\mathbb Z_N^*\\). We lower-bound the probability of the algorithm succeeding in recovering all factors of N given r.\nL193: We show this probability to be very high, by carefully considering cases where r is a multiple of a divisor of \\(\\lambda '(N)\\).\nL194: #### 3.2.4 Notes on analogies with Pollard’s works\nL195: Miller’s algorithm may be regarded as a generalization of Pollard’s \\(p-1\\) algorithm [cite51†17 ]: Miller essentially runs Pollard’s algorithm for all prime factors \\(p_i\\) in parallel by using that a multiple of \\(\\lambda '(N) = \\mathrm {lcm}(p_1 - 1, \\ldots , p_n - 1)\\) is known. Pollard, assuming no prior knowledge, uses a product of small prime powers up to some smoothness bound B in place of \\(\\lambda '(N)\\). This factors out \\(p_i\\) from N if \\(p_i-1\\) is B-smooth, giving Pollard’s algorithm its name.\nL196: Since we only know r, a multiple of some divisor of \\(\\lambda '(N)\\), we grow r to \\(r'\\) by multiplying on a product of small prime powers. This is in analogy with Pollard’s approach.\nL197: ### 3.3 Analysis of the algorithm\nL198: \nL199: A key difference between our modified algorithm in Sect. cite47†3.2 and the original algorithm in Miller’s thesis [cite26†15 ] is that we compute the order of a random g and then add a guessing step: We guess an \\(r'\\) in step 2 that we hope will be a multiple of \\(p_i - 1\\) for all \\(i \\in [1, n]\\), and if not all, then at least for all but one index on this interval, in which case the algorithm will still be successful in completely factoring N.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn exponent one-fifth algorithm for deterministic integer factorisation (https://arxiv.org/html/2010.05450)\nciteturn3view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view1\",\"pattern\":\"Proposition 2.7\"}); Total lines: 569\nL178: ### 2.3. Strassen’s method\nL179: \nL180: The following result, which goes back to Strassen [cite25†Str77 ], may be used to quickly rule out “small” factors of a given integer $N$.\nL181: \nL182: ###### Proposition 2.5.\nL183: \nL184: Given as input a positive integer $M=O(N)$, we may test if $N$ has a prime divisor $p\\leqslant M$, and if so find the smallest such divisor, in time\nL185: \nL186:  | $$O(M^{1/2}\\lg^{3}N).$$  |\nL196: ###### Proposition 2.7 ([cite31†Hit18 , Theorem 6.3]).\nL197: \nL198: There is an algorithm with the following properties. It takes as input integers $N\\geqslant 2$ and $D$ such that $N^{2/5}\\leqslant D\\leqslant N$. It returns either some $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}$ such that $\\ord_{N}(\\alpha)>D$, or a nontrivial factor of $N$, or “$N$ is prime”. Its running time is\nL199: \nL200:  | $$O(D^{1/2}\\lg^{2}N).$$  |\nL201: The algorithm is somewhat involved, and we give only a brief summary here. The idea is to try to compute the orders in $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ of candidates $\\alpha=2,3$, and so on, using a BSGS search up to some bound. If for some candidate $\\alpha$ we fail to compute the order, then the order must be large and we are done. On the other hand, if we succeed in computing the order of a candidate, then this reveals valuable information about the possible prime divisors of $N$.\nL502: Apply Proposition cite62†2.7 with $D\\coloneqq\\lceil N^{2/5}\\rceil\\geqslant 1$. If any factors of $N$ are found, or $N$ is proved to be prime, return. Otherwise, we obtain $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}$ such that $\\ord_{N}(\\alpha)>D$.\nL503: \nL504:   4. 4)\nL505: \nL506: Apply Algorithm cite53†4.2 with the given $N$, $\\alpha$, $m$ and $r$. Return the factors found, or “$N$ is prime”.\nL519: In Step (3), observe that certainly $N^{2/5}\\leqslant D\\leqslant N$. The cost of invoking Proposition cite62†2.7 is $O(N^{1/5}\\lg^{2}N)$, which is negligble. If Proposition cite62†2.7 does not succeed in factoring $N$ or proving $N$ prime, we obtain some $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}$ with $\\ord_{N}(\\alpha)>D$. As $N\\geqslant 10^{9}$ we have $N\\geqslant(\\log_{2}N+1)^{6}\\geqslant\\lg^{6}N$, so $N^{2/5}\\geqslant N^{1/5}\\lg^{6/5}N$ and therefore $\\ord_{N}(\\alpha)>D\\geqslant m$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn completely factoring any integer efficiently in a single run of an order-finding algorithm | Quantum Information Processing | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11128-021-03069-1)\nciteturn3view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view3\",\"pattern\":\"square\"}); Total lines: 521\nL281: The lemma follows from the above argument, as there are \\(\\left( {\\begin{array}{c}n\\\\ 2\\end{array}}\\right) \\) combinations with probability at most \\(2^{-k}\\) each. \\(\\square \\)\nL282: \nL283: By definition q is said to divide u iff \\(u \\equiv 0 \\,\\, (\\text {mod } q)\\). Note that this implies that all \\(q \\ne 0\\) divide \\(u = 0\\). This situation arises in the above proof of Lemma cite52†2 .\nL284: ### Lemma 3\nL285: \nL286: At least two primes are unlucky with probability at most\nL287: \nL288: $$\\begin{aligned} \\frac{1}{2c^{2} \\log ^{2} cm}. \\end{aligned}$$\nL289: ### Proof\nL290: \nL291: The three conditions used to establish the upper bound in the proof of Lemma cite53†1 are independent for any two distinct primes \\(p_i\\). Hence, by the proof of Lemma cite53†1 , we have that the probability of at least two primes being unlucky is upper-bounded by\nL292: $$\\begin{aligned} \\sum _{(i_1, i_2) \\, \\in \\, {\\mathcal {S}}} \\frac{\\log p_{i_1}}{m' \\log m'} \\cdot \\frac{\\log p_{i_2}}{m' \\log m'} \\le \\frac{1}{2 (m' \\log m')^2} \\left( \\, \\sum _{i \\, = \\, 1}^n \\log p_i \\right) ^2 \\le \\frac{1}{2 c^{2} \\log ^{2}{cm}} \\end{aligned}$$\nL293: where we used that \\(\\sum _{i \\, = \\, 1}^n \\log p_i \\le \\log N \\le m\\) and \\(m' = cm\\), and where \\({\\mathcal {S}}\\) is the set of all pairs \\((i_1, i_2) \\in [1,n]^2\\) such that the product \\(p_{i_1} \\cdot p_{i_2}\\) is distinct, and so the lemma follows. \\(\\square \\)\nL294: #### 3.3.1 Runtime analysis\nL295: \nL296: ### Claim 1\nL297: \nL298: It holds that \\(\\log r' = O(m)\\).\nL299: \nL300: ### Proof\nL301: \nL302: By the prime number theorem, there are \\(O(m' / \\ln m')\\) primes less than \\(m'\\). As \\(r < N\\) we have \\(\\log r < m\\). Furthermore, as each prime power \\(q^e\\) in \\(r' / r\\) is less than \\(m'\\), we have\nL303: \nL304: $$\\begin{aligned} \\log r' \\le \\log r + O(m' / \\ln m') \\cdot \\log m' = O(m) \\end{aligned}$$\nL305: \nL306: as \\(m' = cm\\) for some constant \\(c \\ge 1\\), and so the claim follows. \\(\\square \\)\nL307: \nL308: #### 3.3.2 Main theorem\nL309: ### Theorem 1\nL310: \nL311: The factoring algorithm, with the possible exception of the single order-finding call, completely factors N in polynomial time, except with probability at most\nL312: \nL313: $$\\begin{aligned} 2^{-k} \\cdot \\left( {\\begin{array}{c}n\\\\ 2\\end{array}}\\right) + \\frac{1}{2 c^2 \\log ^2 cm} \\end{aligned}$$\nL314: where n is the number of distinct prime factors of N, m is the bit length of N, \\(c \\ge 1\\) is a constant that may be freely selected, and k is the number of iterations performed in the classical post-processing.\nL315: ### Proof\nL316: \nL317: It is easy to see that the non-order-finding part of the algorithm runs in polynomial time in m, as all integers are of length \\(O(m)\\), including in particular \\(r'\\) by Claim cite54†1 . The theorem then follows from the analysis in Sect. cite48†3.3 , by summing the upper bound on the probability of a failure occurring when at most one prime is unlucky in Lemma cite52†2 , and on the probability of at least two primes being unlucky in Lemma cite55†3 . \\(\\square \\)\nL318: By the above main theorem, the algorithm will be successful in completely factoring N, if the constant c is selected so that \\(1 / (2 c^{2} \\log ^{2} cm)\\) is sufficiently small, and if \\(2^{-k}\\) for k the number of iterations is sufficiently small in relation to \\(\\left( {\\begin{array}{c}n\\\\ 2\\end{array}}\\right) \\) for n the number of distinct prime factors in N. The latter requirement is easy to meet: Pick \\(k \\ge 2 \\log n - 1 + \\tau \\) for some positive \\(\\tau \\).\n"
